wgpu-native for Cosmopolitan: experimental headless compute
=========================================================

This directory imports the native WebGPU implementation and a small C
compute check. The current integration targets the Vulkan backend without
a surface, window, or presentation loop. It does not establish general
rendering support or a tested Windows/Linux portability guarantee.

The application uses the native WebGPU C API. The implementation behind
that interface is Rust, with wgpu-core and wgpu-hal executing the GPU work.
Vulkan support still requires an appropriate host Vulkan loader and driver.

Pinned upstream sources
-----------------------

wgpu-native release: v29.0.1.1
Repository: https://github.com/gfx-rs/wgpu-native
Revision: 6aed50955d934ac36049ba8d002034841633ae02

webgpu-headers (the native tree's ffi/webgpu-headers submodule):
Repository: https://github.com/webgpu-native/webgpu-headers
Revision: 673658bc2bd70ec39fc55ebe6bb0173cf6d0a603

IMPORT.json records both immutable revisions, archive URLs and SHA-256
digests, original hashes of every imported file, and the complete omitted
file lists. These hashes describe the pristine imports before local port
patches. Local patches must be reviewed separately from source provenance.

The upstream directory contains regular vendored files. It has no nested
Git repository, Git submodule entry, or CI workflow. Source archives omit
Git submodule contents, so the pinned header tree was imported separately
into upstream/ffi/webgpu-headers.

Pruning is limited to upstream CI/Git metadata and material unrelated to
the Rust library or compute example. The native tree retains Rust and FFI
sources, Cargo metadata, build reference files, licenses, and the upstream
compute/framework examples. GLFW, windowed/rendering examples, repository
maintenance scripts, and their surrounding examples CMake entry point are
omitted. Upstream Makefile/Meson files are retained as reference; their
optional targets can still refer to intentionally omitted examples.

The header tree retains the public API, schema/generator metadata, tests,
documentation, and license, excluding its CI and Git metadata.

License notices
---------------

wgpu-native is available under MIT OR Apache-2.0. Both original license
texts are retained as upstream/LICENSE.MIT and upstream/LICENSE.APACHE.

webgpu-headers uses BSD-3-Clause. Its original notice is retained as
upstream/ffi/webgpu-headers/LICENSE.

examples/compute.c adapts the upstream Collatz compute example and uses
the same MIT OR Apache-2.0 license choice.

Build inputs
------------

The integration build is maintained in build.sh and its associated port
patches. The exact upstream toolchain file selects Rust 1.93. The upstream
minimum supported Rust version is 1.87; those are different statements.
The Cosmopolitan build deliberately selects rust-ape's patched
nightly-2026-07-28 instead. The original Cargo.lock is retained, and the
reviewed Cargo.cosmo.lock is used with --locked for the port. The release's
original lockfile resolves wgpu-core/wgpu-hal/Naga to 29.0.3 despite the
compatible 29.0.1 requirements in Cargo.toml.

Local changes
-------------

The pristine upstream snapshot is never patched in place. prepare.py
verifies it and stages changes under o/webgpu:

  - patches/wgpu-native-cosmopolitan.patch selects a static library only
    and removes the Unix RenderDoc feature, which would use an unadapted
    native-library interface.
  - patches/ash-cosmopolitan.patch changes ash's entry loader only when
    rust_ape_shim is set. The C port owns runtime library selection and
    all function-pointer adapters.
  - patches/wgpu-hal-cosmopolitan.patch rejects Vulkan debug/validation
    flags and custom instance callbacks because their reverse ABI/TLS
    boundary is not implemented in this initial compute target.

Patched crates are downloaded from the immutable crates.io distribution
and checked against the original Cargo.lock checksums:

  ash 0.38.0+1.3.281
  SHA256 0bb44936d800fea8f016d7f2311c6a4f97aebd5dc86f09906139ec848cf3a46f
  Source https://github.com/ash-rs/ash
  License MIT OR Apache-2.0

  wgpu-hal 29.0.3
  SHA256 31f8e1a9e7a8512f276f7c62e018c7fa8d60954303fed2e5750114332049193f
  Source https://github.com/gfx-rs/wgpu
  License MIT OR Apache-2.0

generate_vulkan_signatures.py checks the exact ash dispatch-table hashes
before deriving metadata for all 684 procedure names. Each forward call
uses scalar argument metadata; unknown procedures fail closed. The C
Windows adapter is checked against host GCC ms_abi functions, including
mixed floating point and stack arguments. These tests do not run Windows.

Runtime patches and their provenance are in ../rust_ape/README.cosmo.
New loader/build helpers use MIT; original dependency notices are retained.

The Rust compilation environment must contain:

    WGPU_NATIVE_VERSION=v29.0.1.1

wgpuGetVersion reads that value at Rust compile time. Without it, upstream
returns zero, and the C example's version check deliberately fails.

The C example needs these include directories:

    third_party/wgpu_native/upstream/ffi
    third_party/wgpu_native/upstream/ffi/webgpu-headers

Its executable must link against the ported wgpu-native library. Merely
compiling these headers or linking an ordinary host-specific Rust library
does not validate a Cosmopolitan runtime or a portable APE build.

Executable checks
-----------------

Run the resulting compute executable with --version to call wgpuGetVersion
across the C/Rust boundary. It verifies the actual returned value against
0x1d000101 (29.0.1.1). This path does not initialize WebGPU or a GPU backend.

Run it without arguments for the complete headless check:

  1. Create a WebGPU instance restricted to the Vulkan backend.
  2. Request a Vulkan adapter and device and print the adapter identity.
  3. Compile the embedded WGSL Collatz shader.
  4. Upload [1, 2, 3, 4], dispatch four workgroups, and copy to readback memory.
  5. Check every mapped output against [0, 1, 7, 2].
  6. Release the created WebGPU objects.

The WGSL source is a C string embedded in the executable. No external
shader, GLFW installation, display server, or current directory is needed.
The result identifies CPU/software adapters instead of describing them as
physical GPU validation. A passing software Vulkan test checks functional
execution but does not measure GPU performance.

All checks remain active with NDEBUG. Adapter/device/map callback errors,
uncaptured WebGPU errors, unexpected backend selection, version mismatch,
and incorrect output fail the executable. Callback polling uses a
30-second monotonic timeout instead of an indefinite device wait. A test
runner should still impose a process timeout for a driver that hangs
inside an individual native API call.

Exit status:

    0  The requested check passed.
    2  Invalid command-line usage.
    3  Linked Rust version differs from the pinned release.
    4  Instance, adapter, or device initialization failed.
    5  Execution, shader, mapping, device-loss, or uncaptured API error.
    6  A callback did not complete before the polling timeout.
    7  Compute readback differs from the expected values.

Scope of this port
------------------

The instance explicitly sets Vulkan as its only backend and sets native
instance flags to zero. This avoids enabling Vulkan debug/validation
callbacks, which introduce additional reverse ABI and foreign TLS work
at the driver boundary. Normal WebGPU API validation remains enabled.

Headless compute is the experimental integration target. Window-system
surfaces, swapchains, generic graphics pipelines, and driver debug callbacks
need their own runtime coverage before being represented as supported.
Forward mixed floating-point signatures have host ABI tests, which do not
establish general rendering support. Unsupported calls fail closed.

Successful --version output establishes a narrow C/Rust linkage check.
Successful compute output additionally establishes shader execution and
readback on the reported backend/adapter in the environment where it ran.
Neither result alone establishes that the same APE binary was tested on
another operating system; record any such runs separately.
